<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Wittelsbach</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wittelsbach dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Wittelsbach is a German dynasty that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, as duchy, electorate and kingdom, from 1180 to 1918 and the Electoral Palatinate from 1214. Its members included Emperors Louis IV and Charles VII and kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, and for a time it held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, Holland, Hainaut and Zeeland. The last king of Bavaria, Ludwig III, was deposed in 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach.jpg" alt="House of Wittelsbach" width="320" height="353" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armoiries_Bavi%C3%A8re.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hainaut passed through several dynasties: the house of Flanders, then the Avesnes, who also held Holland and Zeeland from 1299, then the <b>Wittelsbachs</b> from 1345 and the Valois dukes of Burgundy from 1433, before the Habsburgs inherited it in 1482.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Wittelsbachs</b> the duchy's wars were often internal: partitions led to fraternal conflicts, and the Landshut War of Succession of 1503–1505 ended only through imperial arbitration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Wittelsbachs</b> held it until 1803.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Bavaria was proclaimed on 1 January 1806, when Elector Maximilian IV Joseph, of the <b>Wittelsbach dynasty</b> that had ruled Bavaria since 1180, took the title King Maximilian I Joseph.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Ascanian line died out in 1320 the Mark passed to the <b>Wittelsbachs</b> in 1323 and to the Luxembourgs in 1373, and suffered from weak, absent rulers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Holland passed to the Avesnes counts of Hainaut in 1299, the <b>Wittelsbachs</b> in 1345 and the Burgundian State in 1433, amid the Hook and Cod wars.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-holland">County of Holland</a></li></ul></section>
